<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Third Carlist War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1872">1872</a>–<a href="/years/1876">1876</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Third Carlist War of 1872–1876 was a civil war in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in which supporters of the pretender Carlos VII tried to win the throne. The Carlists built a state with its own administration in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a>, the Basque provinces and parts of Catalonia, fighting the governments of Amadeo I, the First Spanish Republic and the restored monarchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-xii">Alfonso XII</a>, which defeated them in 1876.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/third-carlist-war.jpg" alt="Third Carlist War" width="320" height="176" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Francisco Oller y Cestero (1833-1917) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bataille_de_Trevi%C3%B1o.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carlism">Carlism</a> took its name from the infante Carlos María Isidro, count of Molina and brother of Ferdinand VII. When Ferdinand's pragmatic sanction of 1830 set aside the Salic Law, his daughter Isabella became heir instead of Carlos, and the disputed succession led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-carlist-war">First Carlist War</a>. In 1868 the revolution known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glorious-revolution-spain">La Gloriosa</a> overthrew Isabella II, and in 1870 Amadeo I, son of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victor-emmanuel-ii">Victor Emmanuel II</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, was proclaimed King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, a choice the Carlists took as an insult.</p><p>To win support, the pretender Carlos VII promised to restore regional customs and laws, including the Catalan, Valencian and Aragonese fueros abolished by Philip V in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nueva-planta-decrees">Nueva Planta decrees</a>. The state was close to bankruptcy: in 1872 half of the Treasury's revenue went on interest on the public debt.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>A first rising under General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/radaa">Rada</a> failed in the Basque Provinces and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a>, and Carlos VII escaped to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. General Dorregaray took command, and a second rising set for 18 December 1872 succeeded; by February 1873 the Carlist army numbered around 50,000 men on all fronts. Its war parties, among them one led by the priest Manuel Santa Cruz, fought by hit-and-run tactics against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telegraph">telegraph</a> posts, railways and outposts. The Carlists held inland towns such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-seu-durgell">La Seu d'Urgell</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estella-lizarra">Estella</a> and besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bilbao">Bilbao</a> and San Sebastián without taking them.</p><p>Amadeo abdicated in February 1873 and the First Spanish Republic was declared. On 29 December 1874 the pronunciamiento of General Arsenio Martínez de Campos proclaimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-xii">Alfonso XII</a>, and a manifesto by the former Carlist leader Ramón Cabrera in support of the new king weakened the cause. After losing Catalonia, the Carlists made a last stand around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estella-lizarra">Estella</a> and Montejurra, and on 28 February 1876 Carlos VII went into exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The war left between 7,000 and 50,000 casualties. The Basque charters were abolished, customs moved from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebro-river">Ebro</a> to the coast, and conscription became compulsory. Prime Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-canovas-del-castillo">Antonio Cánovas del Castillo</a> then negotiated the first Basque Economic Agreement of 1878, under which provincial councils collected taxes and remitted a share to the state. The defeat opened <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/biscay">Biscay</a>'s mines to companies such as the Orconera Iron Ore Company, and the loss of home rule helped give rise to Basque nationalism and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basque-nationalist-party">Basque Nationalist Party</a> founded by Sabino Arana in 1895.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Third Carlist War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1872, as the Sexenio Democrático unsettled Spain, the traditionalist pretender Carlos VII, Duke of Madrid, launched the <b>Third Carlist War</b> under the banner of God, Fatherland and King.</blockquote><a href="/state/carlist-state">Carlist State</a></li><li><blockquote>The government faced the <b>Third Carlist War</b>, the Cuban Ten Years' War and the Cantonal Rebellion, and its presidents, Figueras, Pi, Nicolás Salmerón and Emilio Castelar, followed in quick succession.</blockquote><a href="/state/spanish-first-republic">Spanish First Republic</a></li></ul></section>
